Keep direct PDF store keys per document (Mervin PDF patch for MuPDF 1.28.5)

This file modifies MuPDF. The Mervin PDF project changed
source/pdf/pdf-store.c on 2026-10-18. scripts/build-mupdf-linux.sh and
scripts/build-mupdf-windows.ps1 apply it to the verified 1.28.5 source
release before building. Check it again on every MuPDF update.

PDF resources such as inline colour spaces, shadings and fonts are
stored under direct pdf_obj keys. pdf_make_hash_key does not hash those,
so fz_find_item and fz_remove_item scan the store and compare keys with
pdf_cmp_key, which is pdf_objcmp. pdf_objcmp ignores which document a
key belongs to, and it compares indirect references by number and
generation only. This has three effects when two documents share one
context:

- Wrong output. Two documents that both use the inline colour space
  [/Indexed /DeviceRGB 1 5 0 R], where object 5 holds different
  palettes, render with the palette of whichever document loaded first.
- Freed entries. fz_find_item can return another document's entry. When
  that document closes, pdf_empty_store frees the entry whatever its
  reference count.
- Freed xref reads. For equal dictionary keys pdf_objcmp looks up both
  documents' xref entries (pdf_get_xref_entry_no_change). The other
  document may be closing on another thread, and pdf_drop_document_imp
  frees the xref before pdf_empty_store removes that document's keys.

This patch makes pdf_cmp_key report keys bound to different documents
(pdf_get_bound_document) as different before it calls pdf_objcmp. Keys
bound to no document, such as names, still compare by value. Indirect
keys are not affected, because their hash already includes the
document. documentsKeepTheirOwnInlineColourSpace in
tests/tst_mupdf_store.cpp checks it.

--- a/source/pdf/pdf-store.c
+++ b/source/pdf/pdf-store.c
@@ -52,6 +52,15 @@
 static int
 pdf_cmp_key(fz_context *ctx, void *k0, void *k1)
 {
+	/* Keys bound to different documents never match. pdf_objcmp
+	 * compares indirect references by number and generation but not
+	 * by document, so equal direct keys from two documents can name
+	 * different objects. For dictionaries it also reads both
+	 * documents' xrefs, and the other document may be closing on
+	 * another thread. Keys bound to no document, such as names, still
+	 * compare by value. */
+	if (pdf_get_bound_document(ctx, (pdf_obj *)k0) != pdf_get_bound_document(ctx, (pdf_obj *)k1))
+		return 1;
 	return pdf_objcmp(ctx, (pdf_obj *)k0, (pdf_obj *)k1);
 }
 
